}","category":"react","kind":"improvement","severity":"medium","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This pull request adds a custom hook for searching employees via a direct fetch inside a useEffect. It currently lacks race condition handling, error handling, loading state, and leverages raw fetch instead of the project's existing RTK Query setup.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean hook structure with explicit typing for the employee results."],"assumptions":["The project standard for data fetching is RTK Query as listed in the project context.","The /api/employees endpoint returns an array of Employee objects on success."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/search/useEmployeeSearch.ts","line":7,"endLine":11,"evidence":"  useEffect(() => {","title":"Race condition in asynchronous search requests","why":"Rapid typing will trigger multiple concurrent requests. If an earlier request finishes after a later request, out-of-order responses will cause the results to display stale data.","fix":"Implement an AbortController or a boolean flag inside the effect to ignore responses from stale requests.","suggestedCode":"  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    fetch(`/api/employees?q=${query}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((res) => res.json())
      .then((data: Employee[]) => setResults(data))
      .catch((err) => {
        if (err.name !== "AbortError") console.error(err);
      });
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [query]);","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95},{"file":"src/features/search/useEmployeeSearch.ts","line":8,"endLine":11,"evidence":"    fetch(`/api/employees?q=${query}`)","title":"Bypass of RTK Query caching and state management","why":"The project context specifies Redux Toolkit and RTK Query for state and data. Bypassing it with a raw fetch duplicates data fetching patterns and loses automatic caching, deduplication, and lifecycle management provided by RTK Query.","fix":"Replace the custom hook data fetching logic with an RTK Query endpoint and generated hook.","category":"state-management","kind":"preference","severity":"medium","confidence":0.9},{"file":"src/features/search/useEmployeeSearch.ts","line":10,"endLine":10,"evidence":"      .then((data: Employee[]) => setResults(data));","title":"Missing error handling for search fetch request","why":"Network failures or server errors will cause unhandled promise rejections because there is no .catch() handler on the promise chain.","fix":"Add error handling to catch network or parsing failures gracefully.","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"medium","confidence":0.9}]}

{"summary":"This PR adds a SaveEntryButton component using an RTK Query mutation to update timesheet entries, with local saved status state.","rating":"acceptable","readiness":"ready-with-minor-changes","strengths":["Disables the button while the mutation is loading to prevent duplicate requests.","Uses semantic role="status" for the saved confirmation message."],"assumptions":["The updateEntry mutation returns a result object that can throw or indicate errors, but unhandled rejection is acceptable if errors are handled globally or outside this component."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/SaveEntryButton.tsx","line":10,"endLine":10,"evidence":"    await updateEntry(entry);","title":"Unhandled mutation error in SaveEntryButton","why":"If the mutation fails, an unhandled promise rejection occurs because there is no try/catch block around the await call, which can break execution flow.","fix":"Wrap the mutation call in a try/catch block or unwrap the promise to handle potential errors appropriately.","suggestedCode":"try {
      await updateEntry(entry).unwrap();
      setSaved(true);
    } catch {
      // handle error
    }","category":"api","kind":"risk","severity":"medium","confidence":0.85}]}

{"summary":"The PR introduces an RTK Query API slice for timesheet entries. However, the mutation endpoint is missing tag invalidation, which will cause the cached entry list to remain stale after adding a new item.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"ready-with-minor-changes","strengths":["Properly types the query and mutation endpoints with TypeScript.","Correctly sets up tag types for cache management."],"assumptions":["The /api/entries endpoint returns the newly created entry upon success."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/timesheetApi.ts","line":13,"endLine":16,"evidence":"    addEntry: build.mutation<Entry, NewEntry>({","title":"Missing cache invalidation on mutation","why":"When a new entry is successfully added via the addEntry mutation, the cached data for getEntries will not update automatically because invalidatesTags is missing. Users will not see their new entry until the page is manually refreshed.","fix":"Add `invalidatesTags: ['Entry']` to the addEntry mutation definition to ensure the getEntries query automatically refetches.","suggestedCode":"    addEntry: build.mutation<Entry, NewEntry>({
      query: (body) => ({ url: "/entries", method: "POST", body }),
      invalidatesTags: ["Entry"],
    }),","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}
